  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Power of Periodic Signals",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the power of a periodic continuous-time signal by converting the average-power limit into $P=\\frac{1}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0}|x(t)|^2\\,dt$ over any fundamental period.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Power of Periodic Signals",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Power of Periodic Signals",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a periodic signal $x(t)$ with fundamental period $T_0$, the cue is repetition: $x(t+T_0)=x(t)$. Periodic-signal power is the average value of $|x(t)|^2$ over that repeating pattern.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t+T_0)=x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the average-power definition from the previous topic. For a periodic signal, the long-time average can be built from repeated copies of one full period, so the simplest picture is a signal that repeats the same level every $T_0$ seconds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because each period is the same, the repeated copies cancel in the average. What remains is one fundamental-period average, so power can be computed from any interval of length $T_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P=\\frac{1}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0}|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The starting point $t_0$ does not matter as long as the interval spans exactly one fundamental period. That is the shortcut the later examples will reuse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Power of Periodic Signals",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Converting Average Power to a One-Period Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For a $T_0$-periodic signal $x(t)$, show how the average-power limit reduces to a one-period integral over $[t_0,t_0+T_0]$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a $T_0$-periodic signal $x(t)$, the cue is repetition: $x(t+T_0)=x(t)$. Start from the average-power limit from the previous section, because the goal is to replace that long-time average with a one-period average.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now partition the long interval into whole periods. Because every block of length $T_0$ has the same squared-magnitude pattern, each block contributes the same one-period integral, so the repetition count cancels when you divide by the total interval length.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P=\\frac{1}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0}|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the one-period shortcut: once the signal is periodic, power is determined by any interval of length $T_0$. The starting point $t_0$ can be anywhere inside a fundamental-period window.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Power of Periodic Signals",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Computing Power from a Given Fundamental Period",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute the power of the periodic signal $x(t)=2$ for $0\\le t<\\tfrac{T_0}{2}$ and $0$ for $\\tfrac{T_0}{2}\\le t<T_0$, repeated every $T_0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the one-period formula from the previous step. Because the signal is already given over one fundamental period and then repeated, compute power by integrating $|x(t)|^2$ over $[0,T_0]$ and dividing by $T_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P=\\frac{1}{T_0}\\int_0^{T_0}|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "On $0\\le t<\\tfrac{T_0}{2}$, the signal equals $2$, so $|x(t)|^2=4$. On $\\tfrac{T_0}{2}\\le t<T_0$, the signal is $0$, so the squared magnitude is also $0$. Split the integral at the point where the waveform changes value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nP&=\\frac{1}{T_0}\\left(\\int_0^{T_0/2}4\\,dt+\\int_{T_0/2}^{T_0}0\\,dt\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{T_0}\\left(4\\cdot\\frac{T_0}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=2\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is $P=2$. The only new work here is recognizing which subintervals are active and which contribute zero; the one-period power formula does the rest.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Power of Periodic Signals",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Choosing Convenient Bounds Over One Period",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A $2$-periodic signal is defined on one convenient period by $x(t)=1$ for $-1\\le t<0$ and $x(t)=3$ for $0\\le t<1$. Compute its power by integrating over the shifted one-period interval $[-1,1]$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that any interval of length $T_0$ works. Here the shifted choice $[-1,1]$ is convenient because it matches the piecewise definition exactly, so each constant level stays on its own subinterval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P=\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-1}^{1}|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Split at the breakpoint $t=0$. On $-1\\le t<0$, $x(t)=1$, so $|x(t)|^2=1$. On $0\\le t<1$, $x(t)=3$, so $|x(t)|^2=9$. The shift changes the bounds, not the power.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nP&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int_{-1}^{0}1\\,dt+\\int_{0}^{1}9\\,dt\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}(1+9)\\\\\n&=5\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the power is $P=5$. The practical rule is simple: pick any one-period interval, then choose the bounds that make the pieces easiest to integrate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Power of Periodic Signals",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Computing Power of a Piecewise Periodic Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute the power of the periodic signal $x(t)=1$ for $0\\le t<1$, $x(t)=-2$ for $1\\le t<3$, and $x(t)=0$ for $3\\le t<4$, repeated every $4$ units.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the same one-period formula, but now the period has three constant pieces. The job is to square each level, integrate on each subinterval, and add the contributions over the full period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P=\\frac{1}{4}\\int_0^4|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "On $0\\le t<1$, the signal is $1$, so the squared value is $1$. On $1\\le t<3$, the signal is $-2$, so the squared value is $4$. On $3\\le t<4$, the signal is $0$, so that piece contributes nothing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nP&=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\int_0^1 1\\,dt+\\int_1^3 4\\,dt+\\int_3^4 0\\,dt\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{4}(1+8+0)\\\\\n&=\\frac{9}{4}\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the power is $P=\\tfrac{9}{4}$. The only bookkeeping is to keep each piece on its own interval, square the levels first, and then average over the full period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
